:: Epigram {"position":"300,50","size":"100,100"}
gothtangzhuang.space is a blog and email based horror interactive fiction about a friend/former lover gone missing. 

The narrator, Tai will be searching for him. It's your job to lead the way. 

Content Warning
- Catholicism mentions Please read further [[here.->CatholicCW]]
- Suicidal ideation, active and passive
- Self-harm mentions (burning, cutting)
- Alcohol use mentions
- Emotional and physical abuse by parents and partner
- Intimate partner violence (IPV)
- Sexual content

Gameplay
- Contains a lot of text
- A few puzzles
- Some graphics

[[Start->NarrativeIntro]]


:: NarrativeGototheLibrary {"position":"700,925","size":"100,100"}
I put on a pair of black jeans that are tight and ripped at the knees and braid my hair in two tails. I then put on a band shirt of a band no knows, not even me, because the logo is completely unreadable. This is the exact same outfit I've worn since my undergrad years in college and I do it sometimes the honor the old me, albeit the new me is getting a little old to dress this way. No one cares though, my body is still trapped in 2017 anyway.

The route I take every week, at least 2-3 times, was a behavioral activation plan I made with an old therapist long ago when I first came back to New York. //Wake up in the morning at 10AM, eat breakfast, take a walk to the train, and then allow myself to people watch until I hit the location of the library. Then I read at least 30 pages of literature in a quiet room.

When I get on the train, I hold onto the pole and feel the tracks beneath my flat feet. I close my eyes and think of my destination to stay in the present moment. The sway and the bump is almost hypnotic.


:: NarrativeLibraryBooks
This place always feels like it's a hell for people who love the company of others. In here, despite it's welcoming golden gates, and its consistently flowing population of readers, writers, musicians, artists, and lonely people, few of these people will make friendly talk and share their epiphanies or their lore. Yet, somehow, this still feels like more company than home. I tend to pick a quiet room all the way in the back of the library because it feels more private despite that no one here cares about the private experiences of others.

I then enter the booth, open up a book, and then read. I do not let my mind wander and I let the prose settle in. The novel of the month is 2666 by Roberto Bolaño and the murals of violence and operas of death and grief are filling my mind. Apparently, I didn't have enough of it growing up. After about 5 pages though, I soon grow restless by the tides of blood driven imagery and I get up to check out some more books.

:: NarrativeLibraryShelves
I've gone through many aisles of this library, often without a single goal in mind. There are a few selections of books that I have on a list, but they are on hold and I don't feel like going through familiarity. The aisles in particular tend to be quite solitary, practically haunting to a level where if someone does come through it feels like an invasion of your own bedroom. The shelves in this area I'm in is unpopular and the lights are flickering every 5 seconds. The books are yellowing and for some the plastic coverings have done their due diligence of protecting. 

One of them seemingly stands out more than the others. In big bold red letters, I see The Man in Green and the author is "Oliver Wong". A name that brings a bit of a flutter to my heart. I clench my ass and the thumping in my chest begin to get audible. For a moment, I believed, that my friend Oliver Wang had finally gotten to written a book about his family history. What a great achievement it would've been if it were true! His work would validate his father, I'm sure. But no, as a cultural and lingual outsider, I would make this mistake. His surname is Wang and he has gotten on my case about that many times in the earlier days of our friendship when our names became important. 

The book may be interesting. I turn it over, I see a man in green uniform and a red star on the cap. His face stoic as one can ever be in a time of war and idealism. His brown eyes are intense and echo the love of his pride. His eyesbrows are thin and his lips are full. It is obviously not my friend, Oliver Wang, nor is it the uncle he described so fervently on one summer afternoon.  

:: NarrativeLibraryOliverBack

It was during my family reunion funny enough. Him and I were sitting on the swings, one a giant tire and the other a plank. It was a few weeks before we would start the Fall semester again for our 2nd year of undergrad college. Oliver came along because even though he wasn't blood, my family practically thought he was an adopted sibling due to how close we were. What they didn't know during that time was that our closeness reached a whole new contrast.

I don't remember what we were talking about but he turned aroud, with his thin brush brows scrunched. An expression I found rather cutesy and he hated when I would say that because when he would do that, it was rarely a good thing coming out of his lips.

"My uncle is dead. The police found him fucking dead in a a parking lot not too far from here. Weren't we just in Pavillion Mall 2 weeks ago?" I recall he literally said this like it was the latest deranged gossip and not that his uncle passed away. It was also strangely placed because I've actually never met his uncle and he was treated like a folklore in the Wang household because of how long he was missing.

He then pulls up a photo he took of a yellowed photograph of his uncle and the stereo music paused as if to let Oliver finally speak of his uncle. He then said "My uncle wore such a cool uniform. It's too bad I didn't get to see him." 

:: IntroBlog {"position":"125,200","size":"100,100"}
Huh. Weird. I never knew space worked like this. I can use the blog editor but when I click on some posts, it's just blank. 

I thought Oliver just made blog themes, didn't expect him to have Windows do his bidding. I've been learning a lot since invading his privacy.


:: Narrative4AMDorm {"position":"275,1175","size":"100,100"}
Olive and I were high, elevating up to Saturn and then crash landing back down to New Jersey when it was over  Our confuzzled feelings were mixed  with alcohol more than the edibles we used at that time. I think I even puked a little in my mouth before we jumped into the bed. I did have two shots of that Chinese wine he gave me and then he finished it off. 

Sometimes this little love scene with Olive comes into my mind at night. Although, rarely these days, but with entering his world again. It feels raw and new.

I recall this happened after our big conversation where we exchanged these feelings under the college bleachers like some damn White people sitcom. It was Olive's plan by the way. 

There is more to this story. There was a red flag or two and I shouldn't have pulled my pants down there. We were just so desperate in our honesty [[I guess.->NarrativeTheBleachers]]


:: NarrativeChildHoodMemory1 {"position":"875,825","size":"100,100"}
I think I remember that one day, that blog post really brings back some memories. I've known Olive for so long but when we hit our puberty years I stopped knowing him. It's hard to explain and to be quite frank he never wanted to explain it me either.

When we got lost in Kids R' Fun, his dad really did slap the shit out of him. It was over something so ridiculous too. Olive and I were at the arcade playing Tekken 3.

He was Hwoarang and I was Lei Wulong. The strobing lights, the muffled SFXs, my hands sweating on the handsticks, and the dried tears on Olive's cheeks from all the hysterical laughing he was doing. I do not remember what was funny during that time, but sometimes he would break down cry laughing for simply being around me. [[>>->NarrativeChildHoodMemory2]]


:: NarrativeChildHoodMemory2 {"position":"1000,825","size":"100,100"}
"Fengfeeenng!" I heard his father call out and I turned and saw his thin figure in the dark, lightened only by the flickers of various arcade games. He was still wearing a fleece even though it was dead in the middle of a hot August.

It only took a minute after him and I locked eyes. His, totally cocked with rage, mine with total doe-eyed child fright. In that 5 seconds, I had realized that we weren't allowed here and Olive never told me that we weren't allowed there.

"Fengfeeenng!" I heard him say again in the exact cadence as before as if he's practiced saying it in the mirror. Things moved quite fast. After all, I think we had to be like around 11 years old? 

I looked down at the neon galaxy stars and purple planets of the carpeted arcade floor as Olive's feet were lifted off of it. I could feel the callused fingers against the moist skin of Olive's cheek.

I wasn't the one receiving the blows but I cried myself to sleep that night.


:: NarrativeDreams {"position":"1375,1100","size":"100,100"}
 I have the same dreams over and over, it is one component of my madness that I like the least. The only ounce of positivity in it is that I can predict the dreams and how they will start, end, and how much they will weight while I'm within them. The heaviest dream involves the survival of a school shooting, despite that I have never had to live one. Thankfully.


:: NarrativeEndGodUpThere {"position":"1225,1150","size":"100,100"}
Olive is still in the hospital and his parents are so shocked, I've been visiting them despite that the search is over. I don't have anything better to do anyway and I don't have any interest going back to the writing desk now.

I am holding the events in my heart and my chest is heavy with the burden of knowing that I had just prevented the most vile shit. Today, I visit Olive in the hospital and his mother was sitting across Olive in the chair. She is quiet, looking down at her son. The father isn't here. So my mother sat next to Mrs. Wang to fill in the role. My mom then says in her typical refusing to read the room fashion:

Mom: "There is a God up there. I know you don't believe in him. But he will come around one of these days."

I lift up the basket behind me on the chair. It is filled with a shower gel, shaving cream, and a couple of candies that Olive likes the most. I give it to his mother who forces a grimace-like smile to accept the gift with her kindness but with hesitation against mine.

I've told the Wangs many times. Yes, I risked my life. No, I don't regret it, except the parts where I kept falling.


:: NarrativeFired {"position":"450,925","size":"100,100"}
This isn't a suprise but it's great way to start my morning. I know this guy had steam coming out of his ears. I'm sorry for not being a human typewriter that will churn out his content drivel without a struggle. He clearly copied and pasted an AI email template and didn't make it personal to him. Speaks to his standards. 

Hello Mr. Tai Rodriguez,

I’m writing to formally notify you that our service agreement dated October 6th 2021 will be terminated effective June 6th 2021. This decision has been made after careful consideration and in alignment with the terms outlined in our contract.

Up until the termination date, I will continue to provide services as agreed upon. After that date, all contracted obligations will conclude. If there are outstanding deliverables, invoices, or materials that need to be completed or transferred, I will ensure they are addressed promptly.

Please let me know if you need any documentation, records, or transition support as we close out our work together.

Thank you for the opportunity to collaborate. I wish you and your team continued success.

Warm regards, 
Bryan Smith

Now, I am for sure fucked. No fortune telling needed. [[>>->NarrativeStillness]]


:: NarrativeHisRoom {"position":"275,1425","size":"100,100"}
There is a scent in here that is familiar. Hair, sweat, and cedar. But there is no warmth of a being here. The cool air entrapped in the empty room pours out and greets my face and goes down my spine.

The light is on and it is a warm yellow glow that reveals his bed being made, his computer chair sheathed into a black carbon fiber desk, and a laptop sits open and dark. He has been missing for 3 months and his mother takes care of the room as if it will coax him back.

The welcome of a clean home isn't enchanting now, which definitely means there's something up.


:: NarrativeIllness1 {"position":"775,1300","size":"100,100"}
I remember when I came down with the illness. When I mean "came down" more like I finally recognized it. I was bedridden for days riding the tide before I could truly gain the acceptance that I was sick as a fucker. 

I could barely move from my bed and I felt like I was swimming in my sheets. I've never had so much sweat come out of my pores and my hair was stuck to my skin. The ringlets that my family always gawked at as being the generational genetic treasure were becoming matted and fuzzed up like caterpillers. 

Honestly, I wish I died that day. It turned out though, I was just really fucked by the heat and I would later come to find out I'm a little more neurally fucked than I thought. [[>>->NarrativeIlness2]]


:: NarrativeIllness3 {"position":"1025,1300","size":"100,100"}



:: NarrativeIlness2 {"position":"900,1300","size":"100,100"}
I was knocked down and slapped silly by a flu and what I would later come to find out was POTS or Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome. I had faded in and out, could barely get out of bed, and watched as the sun tinged my legs from the blinds and the moonlight awashed me overnight.

My mind was in a haze of agonizing dreams the whole time: arguments with my dad, arguments with Oliver, weird sex dreams with Oliver, and then that coach screaming at me. There was no way for me to stabilize my thoughts either. If I got up, it was like someone forced me down with one big push. My hands gripped the blankets and if I turned my world swayed to left and the right. 

Oliver finally came in to check in on me. He was gone at his parents and he didn't get a chance to off load the continued trauma tha night. I was seizing and probably would've been seized by the Gods if he didn't come back.

All I saw was Mary, the Black one in particular. She doted down and her brown eyes were so comforting, so warm. It was so rude to be waken by a shot in my ass. [[>>->NarrativeIllness3]]


:: NarrativeIntro {"position":"300,175","size":"100,100"}
This guy makes me want to drive into the nearby steel powerline but I can't do that because:
1. I don't have a car
2. I respect my neighbors
3. It's near the Chinese bakery where I get the pineapple bread, 

I have to write the words he wants in a mechanical style that is palatable to computers only. To do so, My emotions must be swallowed because truly I want to do them with nurture. Instead, I have to eat shit and write the word "salvage car" just a few more times because I need to pay my rent soon. 

Bryan Smith: I told you to add more keywords and you erased some here and added more there. Use the Toast plugin like I've said. The deadline is this week.

Tai Rodriguez: Can I get an extension because you've asked for revisions twice now and I have other projects to do.  (That's a lie, I'm holding on for dear life on this guy.)

Bryan Smith: I will give you more time but this will be the last time I will commission you. You said you'd be done with this a week ago and you keep not following the guide.

Tai Rodriguez: I will get it done tonight, but how much time are you giving me?

1 hour later, no response. I'm losing money this week I guess and now I regret those gourmet croissants and spending the last few days watching judge shows with Mom.  [[>>->NarrativeIntro2]]


:: NarrativeIntro2 {"position":"300,300","size":"100,100"}
I will hold on a little longer for hope. There isn't a good feeling about this in my throat nor my chest. It hasn't been a good year for me with clients as of late  and the algorithms keep changing their languages so the flow of website posts are getting lower for every agency I work with. I feel that Bryan himself is squeezing my damn adam's apple for making him and I lose money.

Not much I can do to change anything. I can go to sleep and try to reset myself for 30 minutes. It's not like Bryan will change his mind. There's plenty of other bent over, hungry writers he can exploit. I also created this fate, I spent way too many days staring at the green kitchen wall tile, hoping that the content manifests itself.

I get off my writing chair - I mean my supportless stool - and hobble over to my bed. I try not to let the inner critics get to me but boy are they feeling really demanding this afternoon.

"You're a liar."
"You're a fucking loser."
"You're a lazy fuck."

My arguments against these thoughts are: "I'm trying my best with the little nuts I have." The truth is...he never gave me access to the NEW guide until 3 days ago and ignored my emails for most of the week. But it's done and over with, if I go after the rest of my thoughts, I'm going to start ripping and gnashing the comforter on my chest. [[>>->NarrativeIntro3]]


:: NarrativeIntro3 {"position":"300,425","size":"100,100"}
It's almost like my mother knows the inner dread spiral I'm having right now. I can hear the phone vibrations on my night table as I try to sleep. That is not making me feel any better about yet another failure to keep a writing contract.

The pressure of Bryan and the heaviness of life's expectations are weighing me down and I pull the comforter over my face.

The critics begin to quiet down too. [[>>->NarrativeIntro4]]


:: NarrativeIntro4 {"position":"300,550","size":"100,100"}
I sleep under the last blanket my abuela sent to me. It is an emerald color like the rivers of El Yunque and the soft, glowing eyes of my abuelo that I could only see as a squint through his sagging, tanned rivulets of flesh. 

Under the blanket, I dream of places I've been to and don't want to be in. This time, I am in a house that is quite old and unoccupied. I come to an empty dinner table with the utensils and plates set out, I find an empty bed that is made as if I have never laid in it, and an empty living room that has forgotten its purpose of serving entertainment. Neither in the kitchen or the dining room are there people outside of me. Every surface of this living space has a film of dust on it. I could even taste it in my mouth and walking through the room felt like floating through the ocean. My mouth cannot open and my voice could not bring life to the place again.

The taste in my mouth was sour and heavy. It is because I am getting sick with the idea of asking Mother for money and I wake up straight out of this dreamed imagery and vomit into the garbage near my night table. [[>>->NarrativeIntro5]]


:: NarrativeIntro5 {"position":"425,550","size":"100,100"}
It is night time and my body feels like it has been held down by many cinder blocks. Man, I hate when I sleep through a fresh ass day but it had a rough start and writers don't like being told they're bad at their craft. Only thing I can do is make up for the night and wade through with what I have. 

I eat two plantains with an egg. I eat a soughdough with a decaf cafecito. Then I do a secular prayer with the Virgen Maria my mom bought to bless the house despite that I believe in no God. 

It just provides a mindful ritual and it's all I can do now. The prayer consists of no words but instead the flickers of [[my own mind's voices.->NarrativeIntro6]]


:: NarrativeIntro6 {"position":"550,550","size":"100,100"}
Now, it is time to answer my mother, who I had left behind the walls of a cellphone boundary. I've told her that she can text me but I may not respond until 6PM. It is 7PM, this may be a heavy concern for her that I'm not responding. 

I go to my bedroom and I fish out my phone from my night table rubble of random objects.

Mom: "Hello...."
Mom: "Hello!"
Mom: "Hey pa, you got your phone on silent again?"
Mom: "Still working..."
Tai: "I went to sleep after work. I'm okay."
Tai: "One client of mine is being a pain in the ass. I hate to ask this but can we split rent? I don't think I'm going to make it this time."
 A few minutes pass, usually she always has the expectation that I respond sooner. She may have left the phone on her kitchen counter and the TV may be too loud.
 
 At this time of 8PM, she is watching the telenovela that I don't know the name of. The man has a crown of wild curls and smoldering eyes. The love interest is blonde with a beauty mark on her right cheekbone. I sometimes think she believes she is part of the show and there is nothing I can do but wait until she is done with her adjacent to lucid dreaming hobby. I go to the window. [[>>->NarrativeWindow]]


:: NarrativeIntroCarlos [InComplete/Edit] {"position":"450,675","size":"100,100"}
 It is quite a forlorn tune, something meant to tug at your heart strings and I guess eventually rip them out if you're really begging for it. At first, I thought he was just moaning in exhaustion when I first heard the bolero. My Spanish is only medium level efficient because I am quite generationally far from the island by now and my parents I am not close with anymore. It turned out the song is something familiar but I can't pinpoint what it's called. 
 
 -insert lyrics-
 
 It did take me a long while to figure out the words and I have memorized them to plug the words in when I don't understand him. When I look at him, I sometimes think about the many things I want to say to him. My gratitude. My memories. 
 
 [[>>->NarrativeIntroCarlos2]]


:: NarrativeIntroCarlos2 {"position":"450,800","size":"100,100"}
Carlos Hernandez is an owner of an eponymous local bodega. We've known each other before I even made my first efforts to crawl. My mother had a gastronomic ritual on a biweekly basis. She would buy pan sobao from Mr. Hernandez's bread bins and then come home and slather it with cheddar cheese and butter. Alongside, a big jug of black coffee. 

She was pregnant with me when she became his faithful consumer and she reported that he talked to me as well when she ordered Pan sobao. "He wanted to make sure you were a customer too." She said to me once when I was 12 and she was fishing out the last loaf. 

He has aged along with my mother's dedication to the store. You can see it in his face, But not in his hair. I used to see his eyes smile as I entered. but now that I am settled in the colony of my body and he has adapted to the newer and ever hungry markets. I now see those eyes at my front step and witness him drive a truck to deliver his goods.

I think I agree with Mom that he is an angel in disguise. [[>>->NarrativeIntroCarlosEnd]]


:: NarrativeIntroCarlosEnd {"position":"325,800","size":"100,100"}
I give him a wave which I don't normally do. It does feel tense when the song goes on and I, his endearing customer sitting from across, says nothing to him. These buildings are not that far where he can't notice at least the brown blur of my hand waving.

He doesn't notice though. Come to think of it, I might need to delay grocery delivery if I'm losing this client. I have to face my reality and go back to the phone.  [[>>->ReturntoMomPhone]]


:: NarrativeIntroEmailApp {"position":"750,225","size":"100,100"}
This app he's using is for encrypted messages. Looks kind of old too. 2010s kind of app. There's about 3241 unread emails in here. Jeez. What can I even find here.

Looks mostly spam. You can't even escape spam when you want to be more private.


:: NarrativeIntroHome {"position":"100,1400","size":"100,100"}
I moved here in this apartment as an escape. Mother thought it was an ugly little place but I thought it had a lot of charm. Since this home is unwelcoming to my mother, I can covet it to myself along with the financial regrets. Without me depending on her and her depending on me from afar, the cost benefit seems to weigh less in favor of my mind.

The green walls were the real show stoppers and I got orange kitchen appliances to create more synergy with the color pallette. Except the oven, orange ovens only exist for the unworthy wealthy. The apartment building itself has blue exit stairs and has a sibling building right across from it. 

The Blue Sky Apartments were designed by a rich yuppie who took pity on us medicaid brokies and gave us the ugliest places to live. To be fair, it's just 70s nostalgia. 


:: NarrativeMilitaryUniform {"position":"75,900","size":"100,100"}
Underneath this pile text and hyperlinks is a side of Oliver that I'm familiar with but didn't really need to see again. He's wearing a green military uniform with the top opened up to reveal his bare chest. He's holding a fake cigar in his lips in his pink lips with a bleeding crack in the middle. There's this side smirk and glow to his eyes that makes me shudder. He is losing weight and he's always been a real flaco to begin with. A few ribs poke out from underneath the left side of his torso and the dark crescents under his eyes are so deep.

He is also wearing a red star cap that I recall he bought to shock his Dad. "Remind him of those good ol' days!" he said when he was telling me about the Kuomintang for maybe the 100th time since I've known him and buying this exact outfit. 

I can't tell if he's sitting on a toilet lid or a sink but behind him is stark white tile and a mirror that reflects his back, which I can see a few of his thoracic rungs. 

On the right edge, I can see the darkness of a doorway where the mirror may be facing another room. When I squint a little, I can spot a white speck and I cannot tell if it is a face or dust. It looks a bit too elongated to exactly be a stray dot of environmental debris.

I will think of it as a guardian, a spirit. Hopefully nothing malevolent.


:: NarrativeMomWangVM {"position":"125,1175","size":"100,100"}
3 voicemails, 5 missed calls, and 2 "Hello Tai" text messages from a number I don't quite recognize. I put on my earphones, check my surroundings for a moment for any strangers wanting to sit near me. 

Then I listen to the voicemail.

"Hello, this is  Mrs. Wang.  It's about your friend...QingFeng...Please..call me back."

 I could barely hear the callback number if she left one. 
 
 I call back and of course, I go straight to voicemail.


:: NarrativeNeverSayIDidnt {"position":"75,775","size":"100,100"}
I've always told him I could help. But he never accepted the help. If only he'd listen, but it was always like talking to someone at a far down tunnel and then watching as they keep going further down, craning their neck everywhere except behind. 

You can't ever say I didn't say anything Oliver...but I wonder. What do you think of me as a friend? I wonder if I'm good enough. I don't think I was before.


:: NarrativeNewbornBaby {"position":"550,1550","size":"100,100"}
I found Olive wrapped up in a bed sheet in a dark, tight closet. He is much like a newborn baby. He is even covered in blood as if leaving the canal again. His head is completely shaven and his eyes closed. In the middle of his forehead is a wound where the blood canal started. The flow broke into rivulets, forming 4 rivers down his face.

I've always known him as a small guy but not small enough for this to happen. I feel bile coming up my throat because it very much smells like the iron of his blood in here. I turn around and look behind me.

I feel a presence that is heavy and tense. But there is nothing lurking in the kitchen and the TV static is still going. The chirping of crickets will never be the same to me again. It will be the irony of the moment rather than the world [[at sleep.->NarrativeNewbornBaby2]]


:: NarrativeNewbornBaby2 {"position":"550,1675","size":"100,100"}
Dropping my cane was the worst thing I could've done and the smell, the image, just everything here is making me hyperventilate. I lean my face against the cool wall, as if there isn't a possible killer here.

I continue looking at Olive there through my left eye and the more I look I notice the oh so subtle movement of a limb. A hand, maybe his foot. I want to open up the fabric shell and release him. But what if he is not [[a whole person ->NarrativeNewbornBaby3]] anymore?


:: NarrativeNewbornBaby3 {"position":"550,1800","size":"100,100"}
I lean down and start to say his name but instead I just say "Buh" and other mumbled words of "Sorry" and "What the fuck".  I want to kick my own ass. He doesn't seem to budge or crane his heads towards me with all the racket I'm making. 

Then I fall over and hit my face against the wall in the closet. My hair can't keep the friction against the wall so I eventually struggle so more trying to hold my balance and continued falling. 

My last stop is smashing my nose bridge into what feels like the tibias of his legs.


:: NarrativeNewbornBaby4 {"position":"550,1925","size":"100,100"}
At this point, I might as well try to wake him or give him last words before he spiritually leaves. I felt a bit of a jolt in his legs which is a good sign. I then try squirming around and up to his face. Our cheeks touch and his blood smears on to my cheek. He still feels warm, I speak low and slowly into his ear. 

"It's Tai, please give me a hint your alive. I don't care about the guy you're with. Just tell me 'Okay'." 

A lid twitches, a lip parts. I got to get myself off of him.


:: NarrativeNewbornBaby5 {"position":"550,2050","size":"100,100"}
Luckily there's a gap of space to collect myself. I use my hands to elevate myself and then I slide one of the closet doors to the right. I then crawl out of the closet and probably kicked his face on the way out, it will help a bit in this escape.

I then pull Olive out by the shoulders. He is so light weight and I cannot tell if he is dead, sleeping, or just fucking with me. I'm so scared for him, I want to kiss his forehead to see if it would make him think he's at wake and jolt his eyes open.

We are running out of seconds, we cannot perform a theatrical reunion, we have to escape the throat of the monster.


:: NarrativeStagnancy {"position":"700,775","size":"100,100"}
Being here, it feels like nothing has changed. It is true that Oliver hasn't grown fully into a 30 year old body. Remanents of his childhood lingers in his own toy box of a mind. The parents say this is common within our generation. 

At first I denied it. But it is true, we are just not prepared and will continue being in the unknown till we die in our fate.


:: NarrativeStillness {"position":"575,925","size":"100,100"}
Sometimes I, with great passion and with striking chest pain, geniunely hate living here. There is nothing but just the essence of me, my "work", and the dreams I have and can no longer interpret the moment I wake up. 

The only thing that breaks up the routine is my mother when she comes in with bags of plantains and cartons of milk. The window is also my portal to the other world essentially Through it, I see Carlos between his shifts, the sky, and the world continuing to turn as I sit or lay down.

I wonder if there is a meaning to this stillness, if there's a ticket out of this. I breathe in, lay down on the kitchen floor. Yes, the grimey ass floor. I place my hands on my chest and spread my fingers out on my collar bone. I then tap rhythmically from left to right. I say to myself. Nothing because I don't believe in affirmations. Instead I say, go to the shelves. [[>>->GototheLibrary]]


:: NarrativeTaiPastRomance {"position":"475,175","size":"100,100"}
I try to bury this memory deep in me because I want to keep these past parts of Oliver far away from my mind realm. The more I read his posts, the more I remember his different but similar parts. The one where he cries, the one where he cuts, the one where he's so brightly smart, and the one I was attracted to.

Now, I don't ever want to know him beyond posts and an occassional visit where I eat his mom's youtiao and drink green tea. The therapist I had at the time had told me to stay away but something in me keeps visiting.

Perhaps it's because I'm so alone. So stuck in my own purgatories.


:: NarrativeTheBleachers {"position":"275,1300","size":"100,100"}
It was Oliver who made our 10th romantic date a special number and never told me why. Come to think of it. It was a scene from one of those gay, horny mangas he's always reading. He had a plethora of them when he was a teen under his bed until his father came after his collection. I don't know what the manga was called but clearly he was re-enacting the one where he is the blonde guy top and I was the Japanese bottom. 

I just know it. Honestly? At that time, I kind of liked it. I think about it now like it's one of the most core memories of college I want to forget.


:: NarrativeTheLossAgain {"position":"1000,1000","size":"100,100"}
His mother was really feeling the loss again. So I sat down with Mrs. Wang and drank green tea with her in these Euro-Japanese tea cups. In my mind, her sprawling, frantic text messages scroll down and reveal her anguish. My mind tries to tangle itself around the concept of being a mother as my finger pads rub against the blue and gold painted ridges of blossoms.

Oliver Wang, my childhood friend of 20 years, went missing on Lunar Year evening after an argument with his father over politics. 

3 months have passed, police did nothing because Oliver and Mr. Wang have a long history of tussling around in the house, the neighborhood streets, and even in the grocery store. 

Everyone down on Melville Road knows the Wangs' business. I even went down there one Halloween night.  Some old racist White man that Olive has complained about stepped out on his porch. He said "Those Chinese men fighting again? Someone gotta tell them to shut the hell up. They speak English? Maybe they don't understand." 

It turned out they were arguing off and on throughout the day and the sergeant had indeed came previously. And yes, it was over "having too much video game time" and I remember my throat tightening because I kept starting new campaigns. We were having so much fun and I loved to hear him laugh.


:: NarrativeTherapyGames {"position":"750,550","size":"100,100"}
Off and on throughout my life, I've been to a shrink. After getting on Medicaid and getting my own place, I found that I was able to cope better. Before I start my work, I still do a grounding exercise to clear up my mind. 

It is called Categories and the word focus of it also gets me geared up to write as well.

<!----These are mini games for Tai's narrative that occur in the start of his days. The category choices are randomized. Sometimes a specific item will open up a narrative memory for him or he will comment about not knowing where he got these specific words from --->


:: NarrativeTravelMsWang {"position":"875,1025","size":"100,100"}
It's a real long way out. I guess I can take a bit of a trip and give myself a break.


:: NarrativeTruckDiscovered {"position":"1225,1000","size":"100,100"}
It's in the middle of the woods and I see a man burning something in a pit...It looks like clothes? a purse? He is burning some remanents of someone out there. 

I feel my stomach tightening and I clench my jaw to avoid screaming for Oliver. My head also swims and I'm trying hard to keep my body straight. He wouldn't want me to say his name anyway or have anyone hear it.

He always hated his name while I despised his vulnerability that at times was self-imposed or thrusted upon him. 

I try to catch my breathe, fill my lungs up before I can enter the fray. 


:: NarrativeUnlocked {"position":"600,250","size":"100,100"}
I unlock his blog. I can't figure out if this is Olive's OC account where he roleplays or if this is his actual journal. Hopefully not his manifesto.

He doesn't keep a NSFW blog locked and keyed even though he should. So this one is private for a reason. The blog has a nice, stylish UI that still maintains a minimal style. I've always told him that he would make an excellent web designer but he never seems to land a position.


:: NarrativeWangMomCall {"position":"550,400","size":"100,100"}
When I called Mrs. Wang, I couldn't tell if she was at the phone or not. At times it sounded like she was consulting her husband while speaking to me. This whole exchange is making me anxious because she keeps calling me and not saying exactly what. I know it's a language barrier but she is killing me.


:: NarrativeWindow {"position":"325,675","size":"100,100"}
Perhaps the scenary needs to change a little. My eyes need to rest on something other than screens, the words of dead writers, and my mom's concern static filling up my damn phone and my own anxiety about my own concerns transfering to her.

It needs to all be compartamentalized. I pull my computer chair to the screen door and then I open it up to sit on my, in a sense, a levitating "porch" at the sky blue exit stairs.  

The stairs were painted as a project by a dead artist who used to live in my apartment complex. He begged the owner to allow him to paint them like the sky, so he could feel like he was still living here in heaven. I do love the intention and meaning of his art, but there is also a pinch of naivity in it as the rent keeps getting higher. He has been dead for the last 5 years, hopefully, he is enjoying still living here but rent free. 

They keep me elevated but somehow connected to everyone down there on the sidewalks when I can't get out. It keeps me in a division between my thoughts, the people, and me. But someone out there is singing and smoking as a personal ritual for whatever inner experience he has, he does it at this time always. [[>>->NarrativeIntroCarlos]]


:: ReturntoMomPhone {"position":"325,925","size":"100,100"}
I went back to my phone. It is on the night table, quiet and dark. No new notifications arrive, which gets me a little concerned? I look at the time though and it is 11PM. Did I zone out that long on the exit stairs? I know I do it sometimes but I don't remember what I was thinking or doing. 

I go back to my bed and close my eyes. I try to bury the amount of voices I will have to ignore or only partially listen to as I drift into whatever other abyss of my mind. [[>>->NarrativeFired]] 
